  "notes": "Surface derived 2026-07-26 from three primary-source layers, reconciled: (1) the normative spec section (w3c.github.io/mediacapture-extensions#media-capture-html-elements) — the ChromeStatus entry's spec_link; (2) the current w3c explainer (media-capture-elements-explainer.md) — the ChromeStatus entry's explainer_link; (3) the shipping Chromium IDL and implementation (UserMediaElement status=stable at trunk). The nine-item inventory is the full developer-facing surface of the Chrome 151 MVP: the element, two properties (stream, error), one method (setConstraints), one dictionary (HTMLMediaStreamConstraints), three events (stream/cancel/error), and the experimental legacy migration surface (UserMediaElementLegacy flag: type/isTypeSupported/isValid/invalidReason/initialPermissionStatus/permissionStatus/onpromptaction/onpromptdismiss/onvalidationstatuschange) documented as one migration item with an explicit deprecation pointer. Deliberate exclusions, with evidence: the superseded WICG/PEPC draft surface (permissionconstraints JSON child, autostart, enabled, settable stream, streamready/userchangedenabled events) is NOT in the shipping IDL and is documented as superseded, not inventoried; the explainer-proposed autostart attribute is absent from all shipping IDL files and from the implementation source and is noted as not shipped; <camera>/<microphone> are a separate ChromeStatus feature (5153829504024576, CameraAndMicrophoneElements flag, experimental) and out of scope. MDN has no coverage (element + interface 404, 2026-07-26), so this is a full gendn reference (Case B), not a stub. BCD has no HTMLUserMediaElement.json; no upstream WPT coverage was found in Chromium's external/wpt mirror — both recorded as explicit unknowns in the compatibility tables. The page replaces a misplaced v150 page for the same feature id (listing verification 2026-07-26: present in the milestone=151 listing under 'Enabled by default', absent from 150 and 152) and corrects its factual errors (milestone 150->151, OT 144-148 without the unsupported 'extended to 152' claim, Firefox 'Under consideration', and the draft-era API shape). Structural validation cannot prove the prose correct; independent primary-source review remains required."
